olcrtc for OpenWrt
==================

The tunnel is a network interface. Configure it in LuCI under
Network -> Interfaces -> olcrtc, or with uci:

    uci set network.olcrtc.room='https://meet.example.org/<your-room>'
    uci set network.olcrtc.auto='1'
    uci commit network
    ifup olcrtc

The shared key is a file, never a uci value:

    mkdir -p /etc/olcrtc
    # paste the SAME 64 hex characters used by the server
    printf '%s' '<64-hex-chars>' > /etc/olcrtc/olcrtc.key
    chgrp olcrtc /etc/olcrtc/olcrtc.key
    chmod 0640 /etc/olcrtc/olcrtc.key

Client and server must agree on provider, room, transport and key. Disagree on
any one and the link silently never forms -- there is no "wrong key" error, it
simply never connects.

WHAT THIS TUNNEL CANNOT CARRY
-----------------------------
TCP over IPv4, and nothing else. olcRTC's SOCKS5 accepts CONNECT only and its
server egress dials tcp4.

So UDP, QUIC and IPv6 are REJECTED while the interface is up, rather than being
allowed to take the clear WAN path. Consequences worth knowing:

  * QUIC is blocked so browsers fall back to TCP. Sites work; a few are slower.
  * IPv6 forwarding is refused. Set block_ipv6='0' only if you understand that
    IPv6 traffic then leaves unencrypted.
  * Anything UDP-only -- some games, some VoIP, WireGuard inside the tunnel --
    will not work at all. This is a property of olcRTC, not of this package.
  * ping does not work to tunnelled destinations. ICMP is not TCP, so it cannot
    cross a SOCKS5 CONNECT tunnel either. It fails immediately with
    "Packet filtered" rather than hanging. Use TCP to test reachability, e.g.
    `curl https://api.ipify.org` -- which also shows the exit IP.
    Setting icmp_reply='1' makes the local bridge answer pings itself so they
    appear to work, but those replies are synthetic: they also succeed for
    addresses that do not exist, so ping stops being a useful diagnostic.

TROUBLESHOOTING
---------------
    logread -e olcrtc              # both daemons log here
    ifstatus olcrtc                # netifd's view
    ubus call network.interface.olcrtc status

Interface will not come up: check that the key file exists and is readable by
the olcrtc user, and that the room URL is reachable from this router in a
browser on the LAN.
